      "comments": [
        {
          "type": "improvement",
          "priority": "low",
          "timestamp": "2026-06-26T09:42:13Z",
          "note": "MAINTAINABILITY — No issues. All four functions are clearly named, single-responsibility, and fully documented with JSDoc (params, returns, security notes). The resolveCredential() implementation is admirably concise: the two-branch pattern (explicit-ID vs auto-selection) maps directly to the documented strategy. The short-circuit on host === null before filtering prevents a silent false-negative. Code is easy to reason about and modify."
        },
        {
          "type": "improvement",
          "priority": "low",
          "timestamp": "2026-06-26T09:42:13Z",
          "note": "BEST PRACTICES — WHATWG URL API is used correctly throughout: property assignment for token injection (not string concatenation), try/catch guarding every URL construction call, explicit parsed.password = '' to avoid residual password component. The ?? null idiom on Array.prototype.find is idiomatic TypeScript for converting undefined to null. Null-return for both zero-match and multi-match is the correct defensive default — forcing callers to handle the ambiguous case explicitly rather than silently using the wrong credential."
        },
        {
          "type": "improvement",
          "priority": "low",
          "timestamp": "2026-06-26T09:42:13Z",
          "note": "PERFORMANCE — No concerns. All operations are O(n) over the credential array (at most a few dozen entries in any realistic config). No unnecessary copies or repeated parsing. The early return pattern in extractHost() and injectCredentialToken() (check protocol before constructing second URL object) is efficient."
        },
        {
          "type": "improvement",
          "priority": "low",
          "timestamp": "2026-06-26T09:42:13Z",
          "note": "FUTURE CONTEXT — The dual-export of injectCredentials() + injectCredentialToken() is deliberately transitional (WP-005 will remove injectCredentials() call sites). This is acceptable as long as WP-005 actually removes the legacy function and its tests. The Developer's own debt comment in the implementation pipeline provides good tracking. No concern for this WP in isolation."
        },
        {
          "type": "improvement",
          "priority": "low",
          "timestamp": "2026-06-26T09:42:13Z",
          "note": "EDGE CASE — injectCredentialToken() uses extractHost() (which enforces https: protocol) as a guard, then immediately constructs a second new URL(url) in the try block. This is intentionally redundant — the second parse is needed to call .toString() — but it means two URL constructions per call. Acceptable at this scale; not a performance concern."
        },
        {
          "type": "documentation-forward",
          "priority": "medium",
          "timestamp": "2026-06-26T09:42:13Z",
          "note": "[documentation-forward] The module-level file comment for git-credentials.ts accurately states 'Only HTTPS URLs are supported' but does not mention the new resolveCredential() function or the resolveCredential → injectCredentialToken pipeline pattern. A short paragraph summarising the intended call sequence (resolveCredential() → entry.token → injectCredentialToken()) would help future contributors understand how the pieces compose, especially since injectCredentials() (legacy) and injectCredentialToken() (new) coexist in the same file."
        },
        {
          "type": "documentation-forward",
          "priority": "low",
          "timestamp": "2026-06-26T09:42:13Z",
          "note": "[documentation-forward] The Security Auditor's Medium finding (OWASP A01: resolveCredential() with explicit credentialId does not cross-validate the URL host against the credential's configured host) is architecturally important for implementors of WP-005. This constraint should be documented either as a @remarks JSDoc block on resolveCredential() or as a dedicated SECURITY section in the module comment — whichever is more prominent. The note should state: 'When credentialId is supplied, the caller is responsible for ensuring the URL's host is consistent with the credential's configured host. No automatic cross-validation is performed.'"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
